// The vfx package: visual effects drawn by the GPU, beginning with
// particles. A program reaches it with 'import vfx', and gets:
//
// - Emitter (emitter.cone), a stateless particle emitter: a rate and
//   lifetimes, a spawn shape (a point, a disc, a sphere) and a launch cone,
//   forces that each have a closed form (a constant acceleration such as
//   gravity or wind, linear drag, buoyancy that fades as the particle
//   cools, a widening horizontal wobble), and size and colour over life (four
//   HDR colour keys, fades in and out). Nothing is simulated or stored:
//   particle i is born i / rate seconds after its event, and everything
//   about it at any age is a function of (event seed, layer seed, i, age),
//   every random number an integer hash (the noise package's PCG).
// - Event, what starts an effect: a seed, a start, a stop, a placement. It
//   is the record a world shares; every particle follows from it.
// - EmitterParams, an emitter bound to its event at a moment, as the GPU
//   reads it, and particleAt and particleNow, the CPU's twin of the shaders'
//   evaluation (vfx.slang), which tests pin and compare with the GPU's.
// - ParticleRenderer (renderer.cone), which draws emitters through the gpu
//   package as glowing sprites (camera-facing, stretched along their motion
//   across the view) or ribbon trails (each particle's own recent path),
//   added into the render package's HDR frame after the opaque scene, depth
//   tested against it and not written, and before bloom and tone mapping.
//
// A frame, with render's Renderer and Post (the renderer keeping its depth:
// 'renderer.keepDepth = true'):
//
//   mut particles = ParticleRenderer.create(&mut device, TEXTURE_FORMAT_RGBA16_FLOAT, TEXTURE_FORMAT_DEPTH32_FLOAT);
//   imm flame = particles.addEmitter(&mut device, STYLE_SPRITES);     // once
//   ...
//   particles.update(&mut device, flame, &emitter, &event, now);      // each frame
//   renderer.render(&mut device, &mut encoder, &post.hdr, &depth, &camera, &light, &list);
//   particles.render(&mut device, &mut encoder, &post.hdr, &depth, &camera);
//   post.apply(&mut encoder, &target);
//
// Its files are one module, and only this one, the root, imports:
//
//   emitter.cone    Emitter, Event, Particle, EmitterParams, the CPU twin
//   renderer.cone   ParticleRenderer; sprites.slang's SPIR-V
//
// and its shaders, Slang: sprites (the sprite and trail renderers) and the
// module vfx (the evaluation) it imports, which imports the noise package's.
//
// DETERMINISM. The hashes, the spawn indices and every count are integers,
// the same everywhere. The positions use exp, sin, cos, square roots and
// divisions, which a GPU computes to looser bounds than the CPU, so a
// particle's place on the GPU is close to the CPU's, not identical; the
// parity test measures how close.

mod vfx;

import libc;
import geomath use *;
import collections use *;
import noise use pcg4d, toUnit, toSigned, UVec4;
import gpu use *;
import render use Camera;
